              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The term **\"iCloud control\"** can refer to a few different things depending on your device and what you are trying to accomplish. \n\n---\n\n### 1. iCloud for Windows (formerly *iCloud Control Panel*)\nIf you are using a Windows PC, Apple provides an official application to control and sync your iCloud data with Windows. While older versions were named **iCloud Control Panel**, the modern version is called **iCloud for Windows**.\n\n* **What it does:** Allows you to sync your iCloud Photos directly to the Microsoft Photos app, view your iCloud Drive in File Explorer, sync Safari passwords using the iCloud Passwords extension, and access iCloud Mail, Contacts, and Calendars through Microsoft Outlook.\n* **How to get it:** You can download it directly from the **Microsoft Store** on Windows 10 or Windows 11.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Managing (Controlling) iCloud Settings on Apple Devices\nIf you want to control your iCloud storage, back up your devices, or toggle which apps sync to the cloud, you can do so directly through your Apple device's settings:\n\n* **On iPhone or iPad:**\n  1. Open the **Settings** app.\n  2. Tap your **Name** (Apple Account) at the very top.\n  3. Tap **iCloud**. \n  4. From here, you can tap *Manage Account Storage* to free up space, or toggle specific apps (like Photos, iCloud Drive, Notes, and Messages) on or off.\n* **On Mac:**\n  1. Click the **Apple Menu (\uf8ff)** in the top-left corner and select **System Settings** (or *System Preferences* on older macOS versions).\n  2. Click your **Name** (Apple Account) at the top of the left sidebar.\n  3. Click **iCloud** to control your synced apps, storage limits, and advanced features like Private Relay or Hide My Email.\n* **On the Web:**\n  * You can sign in to **[iCloud.com](https://www.icloud.com)** from any web browser to view, delete, or upload files directly.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \"iCloud Control\" (Third-Party macOS Utility)\nIf you are looking for **iCloud Control** (specifically spelled this way), it is a popular open-source, third-party utility for Mac users hosted on GitHub (created by developer *Obbut*). \n\n* **What it does:** macOS natively handles iCloud Drive sync automatically, which means it sometimes downloads or uploads files without your manual consent. This utility adds a menu option to the macOS Finder toolbar, allowing you to manually choose which files or folders are kept locally on your hard drive and which ones stay purely in the cloud (selective sync).\n* **How to get it:** You can download it via GitHub or install it using Homebrew Terminal on Mac with the command: `brew install --cask icloud-control`. *(Note: Be aware that Apple has introduced similar native options in newer macOS versions, which has made this third-party tool less necessary for newer operating systems).*",
